    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Already a famous warrior and the main priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huitzilopochtli">Huitzilopochtli</a>'s temple, Moctezuma was elected tlatoani of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1502 or 1503 and fought some 73 campaigns, many of them to crush rebellions, carrying the empire to its greatest size.</li><li>At home he centralised power in his person, barred commoners from his palace and high office and raised tribute, policies that left subject peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totonacs">Totonacs</a> and Chalco ready to ally with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</li><li>Received as a guest in 1519 and soon held prisoner in his own palace, he died in June 1520 in circumstances the sources tell differently; Spanish writers later cast him as weak and superstitious, a picture many historians now treat as post-conquest myth.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A Spanish soldier with Cortés reported that at his meals Moctezuma drank nothing but chocolate, served in a golden goblet, flavoured with vanilla or chili and whipped to a froth.</p></aside>
    </section>

